Twist Hacking Guide
====================

Copyright (C) 2026 Chris Burdess


Coding style
------------

ISO C99, enforced with -std=c99 -pedantic.  Do not use C11 features
(_Static_assert, <stdatomic.h>, _Alignas, anonymous unions, _Generic).
The reactor's cancel flags and command queue use the __atomic builtins
through src/reactor/atomic.h, which is valid in C99.  Follow the GNU Coding
Standards: GNU indentation, a space before the opening parenthesis of
calls and declarations, return type on its own line in definitions, and
no tabs.  Every public symbol starts with twist_ (types and functions)
or TWIST_ (macros).  The library exports nothing else.

Every source file carries the GPLv3+ notice used in the existing files.
Every translation unit includes config.h first.

Source layout
-------------

libtwist is one library.  src/ holds the public-facing pieces (the
internal header, init, i18n, and the twist command).  The rest is
split by layer, and a new scheme is a new directory:

  src/reactor/   loop, connection, poll, timers, storage
  src/engine/    URL, transfer, runtime, proxy, scheme table
  src/dns/       DNS client: messages, DoQ/DoT/DoH/UDP order, resolver
  src/auth/      SASL clients, HTTP Basic and Digest, MD5, SHA-256
  src/http/      HTTP/1, HTTP/2, HTTP/3, cookies
  src/ftp/       FTP and FTPS
  src/file/      file://
  src/tls/       quickstep TLS and QUIC under the connection

Private headers stay next to the sources that use them.  Everything
else includes twist-int.h.

Push parsing
------------

Protocol handlers are state machines fed by Endpoint.receive.  The
handler returns how many bytes it consumed.  The connection keeps the
unconsumed tail in one inbound buffer and presents that tail again in
front of the next read.  That buffer is capped at 256 KiB
(TWIST_IN_CEILING).  A socket read asks only for the room left under
the cap, so one readable event cannot allocate past it.

Returning 0 means the tail is an incomplete token and more bytes are
required.  The connection keeps reading until the cap.  If the cap is
full and the handler has not paused, the token does not fit: the
connection fails and the buffer is released.  A missing delimiter
therefore cannot grow memory and cannot pin the cap for the life of
the process.

Backpressure is pause_read and resume_read.  Pause drops read interest,
leaves the buffered tail in place, and lets the TCP window close.
resume_read presents that tail again.  A zero return is not
backpressure.

Output has the same rule: an upload (or any body a handler sends from a
source) is never queued whole.  A connection holds about a chunk of what was
sent and not yet written (TWIST_OUT_HIGH, 16 KiB, a TLS record); after a send
twist_endpoint_full says whether it does, and the producer then stops, holding
only the chunk it has, and goes on from the endpoint's on_write_ready (set it
with write_user, and guard against the callback arriving from inside the send
that causes it).  A source that is one stream of bytes is a push source
(twist_connection_attach_source): the producer calls twist_connection_push
for each chunk and push_end when it is over, and does not wait to be asked.
The connection calls pause when it holds TWIST_OUT_HIGH and resume from the
write path when it holds less; between the two the producer pushes nothing
more, apart from a chunk already under way.  The chunk is released when the
connection has no further use for it, and the producer reuses its buffer only
after that.  The end callback is called exactly once, by whichever of the
producer and the connection ends it first.  twist_connection_stream remains,
as an adapter that pulls from a read callback on the loop thread, until the
handlers have moved to push sources.  A handler that sends frames of a
protocol with its own flow control (HTTP/2's windows, QUIC's) stops for both.
The source is read as it is sent, never to its end first; if the data has to
be sent again the source is restarted (twist_connection_source_restart,
TWISTOPT_SEEKFUNCTION) or the transfer fails with TWIST_ERR_REWIND.  A new
scheme with an upload does this from the start.

The body of an upload that comes from the application is a push source on the
transfer (twist_transfer_upload), not a read callback that a handler calls.
A handler attaches as the consumer (twist_upload_attach): it is given each
chunk, pauses the producer while it cannot send (twist_upload_pause), and
resumes it (twist_upload_resume).  Where the application gave a
TWISTOPT_READFUNCTION the producer is an adapter that calls it on the loop,
only while the consumer has not paused it.  HTTP/1 frames what is pushed
(chunked encoding, or a known length that is never sent past), and the
connection pauses the producer at TWIST_OUT_HIGH.  The HTTP/2, HTTP/3 and
WebSocket pumps keep one chunk, ask for the next when they can send it, and
pause the producer between.  A handler never calls read_cb.  FTP, SMTP and IMAP
send the upload with twist_relay_start, which queues each chunk on the
connection (through the handler's own chunk function if it has to transform
it), pauses the producer while the connection is full, and ends once, with
twist_upmeter following the body bytes the connection has written.  A handler
that gets a source from memory uses twist_upload_from_memory, and one that
resumes at an offset uses twist_upload_skip.  An operation that returns an
error from a relay, or calls twist_relay_done or twist_relay_abort, leaves the
relay gone and does not touch it again.

Body bytes are sent with twist_connection_send_body (twist_connection_push
does so) and framing with twist_endpoint_send.  twist_connection_body_written
is how many body bytes the socket has accepted, which is what the progress
meter shows for an upload: not those still queued, and not chunk headers or
protocol commands.  sftp and SMB count a write when the server acknowledges
it, and keep their own counts.

A sink is the other direction.  A handler gives what it has received to
twist_sink_feed and returns the count from receive; a sink that takes fewer
bytes than it was given has paused, the connection pauses the read, and the
tail is presented again when the sink calls twist_sink_resume.  HTTP/2 and
HTTP/3 pause one stream and do not use the connection-level pause.  An HTTP response
body is paused with twist_http_pause_body: on HTTP/1 the transfer's own
connection stops reading, and on HTTP/2 only the stream is paused (its window
is not given back, so the server stops when it has used it) while the
connection and its other streams go on.  Data already on its way is held, no
more than 2 MiB, and given on twist_http_resume_body.  Pausing a whole
multiplexed connection because one stream's consumer is slow is the mistake
this avoids.

A handler gives the body to the application with twist_deliver and never calls
the write callback itself: a callback may return TWIST_WRITEFUNC_PAUSE, and
twist_deliver then holds what was not taken and what follows, and calls the
transfer's pause function (set it in attach to stop the peer: pause the
connection's read, or the stream).  Its resume function undoes that.  Where a
handler has no such function the held body is the bound on what the peer can
make the transfer keep.

Parsers do not copy the tail.  A field that must be remembered across
calls is a fixed-size scratch with its own hard maximum (the HTTP/1
line buffer is one).  Bytes copied into that scratch are counted as
consumed so they are not stored twice.  Nothing in the parser allocates
in proportion to the peer's input.  Parsers never block and never read
a socket themselves.  One forward pass.  Bodies are delivered as they
parse; do not buffer a whole message.  Unit tests feed the same input
whole and in small chunks, including 1-byte splits, and retain whatever
the parser did not consume.

What not to put in the loop
---------------------------

The loop, the poll backend and the connection do not mention HTTP.
A new scheme is a twist_scheme row and a ProtocolHandler (or a
datagram handler, or a storage-pool job).  Listen and dial both pin
the endpoint to one loop for life.  Secondary connections of one
transfer (an FTP data socket, another HTTP/3 stream) stay on that loop.

quickstep
---------

TLS and QUIC state machines come from quickstep.  Twist owns the
socket, the monotonic clock and the poll timeout.  gq_tlsauto sits
under the connection so the handler sees plaintext.  A QUIC socket is
one UDP fd on that same loop: recvmmsg, gq_endpoint_recv, gq_endpoint_send
until it yields nothing, sendmmsg.  Each stream is an Endpoint.

Warnings
--------

The tree must build with zero compiler warnings.  Fix the cause; if a
warning is genuinely unavoidable, add a narrow, commented suppression.

Testing
-------

  make check              # unit tests
  make check-integration  # peers on the network, with what the host has
  make check-lab          # the same, in a podman container
  make distcheck          # before every release

make check runs the programs in tests/, one per subsystem.  It is for CI and
it is the unit tests: a test feeds a handler or a state machine bytes and reads
what it sends, through mocks, with a virtual clock, and does no I/O of its own,
not even on loopback.  Anything that needs a peer to answer goes in
integration/.  integration/ is not a subdirectory of 'make check'.  (The unit
tests do not all keep to this yet: see "Tests" in TODO for those that still
open a loopback socket.)

Integration tests run against real peers (python, node, nghttpx, OpenSSH,
vsftpd, Samba) and are never run automatically.  They skip with exit 77 when a peer is
absent, so 'make check-integration' works on a machine that has some of them.
The reference environment is a podman container:

  make check-lab                         # build the image, run every test
  integration/lab.sh TESTS="ftp.sh"      # some of them
  integration/lab.sh --shell             # a shell in the lab

It is the "integration" stage of ci/Dockerfile: the build of twist that CI
makes, a clean Ubuntu with every peer installed, run as an ordinary user with
no network but the container's own loopback.  Peers run inside it, so no test
depends on the version of anything on the developer's machine.  In the lab a
skip is a failure (integration/lab-run.sh), since every peer is there; a test
that must skip there is named in LAB_ALLOW_SKIP.  Because the container is root
of its own namespace, a test may do what a developer cannot: replace
/etc/resolv.conf, or listen on a privileged port.  A new peer is a package in
that stage, and a new test is a script in integration/ that starts it on a
loopback port, as the others do.

The progress meter and bar must match curl's output, since scripts may read
it: the layout and the arithmetic are in tests/test-progress.c and
tests/test-bar.c, with a clock the test sets, and the lines curl wrote for the
same numbers; what the tool shows and
when in tests/cli-progress.sh, on file:// URLs; the sizes over HTTP in
integration/http-progress.sh, against a server of python's.  The environment variables are tested in tests/test-proxyenv.c (which proxy,
and the no-proxy list), tests/test-env.c (the trust store, the key log and
qlog), integration/env.sh (the proxies against a server of python's) and
integration/proxy-https.sh (https proxies, of node's, in front of an origin of
python's; the sftp case is in integration/ssh-openssh.sh; ftps, with a
server of python's that takes the data connection at RETR and starts TLS on it
after its 150 reply, as vsftpd does, is integration/ftps.sh).  HTTP/3, direct and
through a CONNECT-UDP proxy, is tests/test-template.c and tests/test-masque.c
(units, with the QUIC glue under tests/test-env.c) and integration/h3-masque.sh,
against a peer of aioquic's (h3-peer.py: an origin and a CONNECT-UDP proxy);
that script also has the origin open a bidirectional stream, which ev_stream_data
in quic.c must end the connection for.  Discovered resolvers (RFC 9462) are
integration/dns-ddr.sh, with a nameserver of python's (dns-peer.py) on port 53,
which only the lab can have.  Tests and
their peers are written in C or Python, the languages the GNU Coding
Standards name.  To
compare with curl, run both on the same URL with COLUMNS set and 2>&1 >/dev/null,
and look at the bytes (| od -c or | cat -v).  A hand test of the tool on a
terminal shows nothing unless the body goes to a file.

Continuous integration
----------------------

.github/workflows/ci.yml runs "make check" on every push to a branch, built
with gcc and -Werror, in the "check" stage of ci/Dockerfile.  The integration
tests (make check-lab) and the fuzzers are not run there.  The Dockerfile builds what twist
needs and Ubuntu 24.04 does not have (libgcrypt 1.12.4, quickstep and
foxtrot, the last two from the tip of their main branches), so the same build
can be had anywhere with docker, with libbrotli and without it:

  docker build --target check -f ci/Dockerfile .
  docker build --target check -f ci/Dockerfile \
    --build-arg TWIST_CONFIGURE=--without-brotli .

Fuzzing
-------

  fuzz/fuzz.sh list                  # the targets
  fuzz/fuzz.sh run -t 60 [-l] [T...] # fuzz each; -l looks for leaks too
  fuzz/fuzz.sh replay T FILE...      # an input that failed, again
  make check-fuzz                    # the inputs that once found a bug

Every parser that takes bytes or text from outside has a harness in fuzz/
(harness-NAME.c), written as for libFuzzer: LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput, and if it
likes, fuzz_seeds and fuzz_tokens.  There is no libFuzzer or AFL on every
machine, so fuzz/engine.c is a small coverage-guided fuzzer that needs only
gcc: the script compiles the library again, in fuzz-build/, with
-fsanitize=address,undefined and -fsanitize-coverage=trace-pc.  With clang a
harness builds with -fsanitize=fuzzer and engine.c is not needed.

A harness frees what it makes, so a leak is a finding.  An input that
fails is written to fuzz-build/artifacts/NAME; when the bug is fixed, put
the input (they are small) in fuzz/regress/NAME/ with a name that says what
it was, write the ordinary test as well, and 'make check-fuzz' will keep it
fixed.  Fixing comes first-test-first as for any bug: a fuzzer finds the
input, the test says what should have happened.

What is fuzzed is what the network or the user can send to twist's own
code: URLs, globs, variables, quote commands, proxy replies, HTTP/1, HTTP/2,
HTTP/3 and the header compressions, DNS messages, content codings, cookies,
Alt-Svc, FTP, SMTP, POP3, IMAP, MQTT, LDAP, TFTP, DICT, Gopher, WebSocket,
SASL and Digest, and the cutting of mail text.  SSH is foxtrot's and the TLS
and QUIC stacks are quickstep's, and are fuzzed there.

Copyright
---------

The project intends to become part of the GNU system with copyright
assigned to the Free Software Foundation.  Until then, keep the notice
"Copyright (C) YEAR Name" in each file, and do not incorporate code
whose licence is not compatible with GPLv3+.

Documentation and translation
-----------------------------

The manual is Texinfo (doc/twist.texi); update it when the public API
or user-visible behaviour changes.  Build it with "make info".
doc/twist.1 is the short man page.

Every string a person might read (twist_strerror, the CLI, diagnostics)
is wrapped in _() and its source file is listed in po/POTFILES.in.  Do
not translate RFC header names or scheme tokens.  A transfer diagnostic
on the CLI has the form "twist: (N) message" on stderr.

Building
--------

  ./bootstrap
  ./configure
  make
  make check

quickstep and foxtrot must already be installed, so that pkg-config can
see quickstep.pc and foxtrot.pc.  foxtrot needs libgcrypt 1.12.4 or later
and configure checks the pkg-config version, the header and the library
the loader gives a program.  zlib is required.  libbrotli is optional: with it
(libbrotlidec and libbrotlienc, found by pkg-config, HAVE_BROTLI in config.h)
twist decodes and codes br, and without it br is not a known coding, so the
default Accept-Encoding is "gzip, deflate".  Both builds are tested: CI builds
with --with-brotli and with --without-brotli.  liburing is optional too (found
by pkg-config, HAVE_LIBURING in config.h): on Linux, with it, the file I/O of
transfers goes through io_uring (reactor/uringio.c) when the kernel and the
sandbox allow a ring, and through the storage pool when they do not, or when
TWIST_NO_URING is in the environment.  Docker's and podman's default profiles
refuse io_uring_setup, so a container shows the pool; to see the ring run it
with --security-opt seccomp=unconfined (and label=disable under SELinux).
test-fio runs its tests through both and says which it used.  No dependency
other than quickstep, foxtrot (and so libgcrypt), zlib, libbrotli, liburing, and
GNU readline is added without explicit approval.  Readline links into the twist
program only.

The body of a download to a file is handed to the writer in pieces of 256 KiB
(TWIST_FIO_WCHUNK_DEFAULT in twist-int.h).  To find a better size on a machine
or a file system, set TWIST_WRITE_CHUNK in the environment: a number of bytes,
with k or m after it for KiB or MiB, between 16k and 4m (outside, it is held to
the nearest; what is not a size is ignored).  It is read once, by the first
write, and ignored by a setuid program.  Each write holds up to four buffers
of that size, and the sink one more.  bench/run.sh passes the environment to
twist, so TWIST_WRITE_CHUNK=128k bench/run.sh compares sizes.
